<div class="section">
  <p class="eyebrow">The pattern</p>
  <h2>Why this exists</h2>

  <p>I founded Third Mind Learning after years of coaching students through the SAT and similar exams. More than 150 of them so far. I&rsquo;m a Certified HeartMath&reg; Trainer.</p>

  <p>The approach came out of a pattern I couldn&rsquo;t ignore: capable students who understood the material but faltered under pressure. They could solve problems at home. Under timed conditions, something shifted. Breathing changed. Thinking narrowed. Doubt crept in.</p>

  <p>The issue wasn&rsquo;t knowledge. It was state.</p>

  <p>My own training is in physics and mathematics, and I pair that with evidence-based nervous system regulation to help high-performing teens access their full capability when it matters most. Years of leadership work in technology and business are where the analytical and strategic side of how I coach comes from.</p>

  <p>My students regularly see score increases of 100&ndash;150+ points. That doesn&rsquo;t come from more drills. It comes from training the internal conditions that let a teen think clearly under pressure.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <p class="eyebrow">Why this work</p>
  <h2>The kid I was trying to reach</h2>

  <p>I do this because I want to be the guide for young people that I never had.</p>

  <p>There&rsquo;s a specific kind of frustration in watching a capable kid decide they&rsquo;re not capable. It happens quietly, over a few test scores, and by the time anyone names it the belief has already set. Most of what&rsquo;s offered to that kid is more of the thing that isn&rsquo;t working.</p>

  <p>I also do it because mathematical thinking and analytical reasoning are among the most valuable and fastest-disappearing skills around, and equipping a teenager with them opens doors that nothing else does.</p>

  <p>And because of where these students are standing. Sixteen, seventeen, deciding what they believe about themselves. If someone gets on a different trajectory right now, the effect doesn&rsquo;t stop with them.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <p class="eyebrow">The method</p>
  <h2>What I actually teach</h2>

  <p>The technique is not mine. It comes from the HeartMath Institute, a nonprofit research organization founded in 1991 that has spent thirty years studying heart-brain communication, heart-rate variability, and performance under pressure. Their work is peer-reviewed, published in journals including <em>Applied Psychophysiology and Biofeedback</em>, and has been delivered to the military, hospitals, and school systems.</p>

  <p>What I bring is the bridge: the same physiological training, applied specifically to a teenager sitting down to a timed test, combined with the content and strategy that test actually requires. As far as I can tell, nobody else is pairing the two.</p>

  <p>Three things make it credible rather than another relaxation pitch:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>It&rsquo;s measurable.</strong> Heart-rate variability is an instrument reading, not a feeling. A student can watch their own rhythm change on a screen.</li>
    <li><strong>It&rsquo;s fast.</strong> Two to three minutes, no equipment required.</li>
    <li><strong>It explains the failure.</strong> Roughly 85&ndash;90% of the nerve fibers between heart and brain carry signals upward. That&rsquo;s why &ldquo;just calm down&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t work, and why starting with the body does.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <p class="eyebrow">How I work</p>
  <h2>Calm, specific, no hype</h2>

  <p>If your teen isn&rsquo;t a fit for this, I&rsquo;ll tell you on the call instead of taking the engagement. A student with real content gaps needs something else first, and I&rsquo;d rather lose the work than sell the wrong thing.</p>

  <p>I don&rsquo;t promise a number. Not every student jumps 150 points. What I can say is that when students do the work, they improve, because we&rsquo;re removing interference instead of adding volume.</p>
</div>

